# Example .stripeauditignore — suppress known/accepted stripe-audit findings.
#
# Copy this file to your repo root (next to package.json). stripe-audit reads
# ./.stripeauditignore automatically; the same patterns can also be passed with
# repeatable --ignore <pattern> flags. Suppressed findings are still counted in
# the report's "suppressed" tally — they just don't affect the score or the
# --fail-on exit code.
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style: one pattern per line; blank lines and lines
# starting with `#` are ignored. Three forms:
#
#   RULE_ID                 suppress EVERY finding of that rule
#   :resource_id            suppress EVERY finding on that resource (also `*:resource_id`)
#   RULE_ID:resource_id     suppress ONLY that rule on that one resource (narrowest)
#
# Run `npx stripe-audit --list-rules` to see every rule id.
# An unmatched pattern is reported as an info-level warning on stderr and never
# changes the exit code, so stale suppressions surface instead of hiding silently.

# ── By rule id ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# We intentionally ship unbranded receipts while the brand assets are in review.
UNBRANDED_RECEIPTS

# Statement descriptor is set at the platform level, not per-account — accepted.
STATEMENT_DESCRIPTOR_MISSING

# ── By resource ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A legacy webhook endpoint kept alive for one partner; suppress all its findings.
:we_00000000legacyEXAMPLE

# ── By rule + resource (narrowest — preferred) ─────────────────────────────────
# This one archived price is deliberately missing a lookup key; don't flag it,
# but DO keep flagging the rule everywhere else.
PRICE_NO_LOOKUP_KEY:price_00000000archivedEX

# A single product legitimately has two active prices (grandfathered plan).
MULTIPLE_ACTIVE_PRICES_PER_PRODUCT:prod_00000000grandfEX
